  "id": "ch0-the-zukertort",
  "title": "The Zukertort and its idea",
  "intro": "The Colle-Zukertort is a system, not a sequence to memorise. Eight moves build the same little structure against almost anything Black does after 1...d5: d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3, b3, Bb2, Nbd2 and castle. Then the position tells you which of two plans to take.\n\n## Why a second system\n\nYou already play the London, and this is the same idea seen from the other side. Both put a knight on e5, both attack with f4, and both keep the theory small. They differ on one decision: which bishop leaves the pawn chain, and what holds the centre behind it.\n\nIn the London the dark-squared bishop goes OUT to f4 before e3 shuts it in, and the c3 pawn holds d4 from the side. In the Zukertort it goes to b2, behind its own pawns, where it looks down the long diagonal at e5 and g7, and the knight on d2 holds the centre instead. The London bishop watches Black's kingside from the edge. The Zukertort bishop watches it through the middle of the board.\n\nThat is a genuinely different structure, and learning it teaches you both. Play the London and you will notice what the pawn on c3 costs. Play this and you will notice what the bishop on b2 cannot do.\n\n## The two plans\n\n**Ne5 and f4.** The knight lands on e5 held by the d4 pawn and the b2 bishop, the f-pawn comes up behind it, and the rook lifts to f3 and swings to h3. This is the attack the system is known for and most of this course is about it.\n\n**dxc5 and c4.** When Black organises to trade on e5, which usually means the queen to c7 with a bishop on d6, the outpost is not worth having. Take on c5 instead, play c4, and use the bishop on b2 against a queenside that has just been opened.\n\nKnowing which of the two the position wants is the whole skill. Everything else in this course is the same setup with that one question asked in different positions.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying Ne5 because the square is there, when Black can trade on it and be happy. Recapturing on e5 with the d-pawn when the f-pawn would open a file. Putting the bishop on d3 against a kingside fianchetto, where it bites on nothing. And starting the attack before the knight on d2 and the rook on f1 have joined in.",
